Input: {"question": "When was Robert Hutchinson elected to a public office?", "source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d00", "start": 0, "end": 55, "text": "Ann Gray, with whom he had one son and three daughters."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d00", "start": 56, "end": 167, "text": "In 1890, he returned to Geraldton with his young family, and was elected to the Geraldton Town Council in 1892."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d00", "start": 168, "end": 263, "text": "He was made a Justice of the Peace in 1898, and became Mayor of Geraldton from 1898 until 1900."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d00", "start": 264, "end": 438, "text": "At a by-election on 24 July 1900 following the resignation of Richard Robson, Hutchinson was elected to the Western Australian Legislative Assembly for the seat of Geraldton."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d00", "start": 439, "end": 570, "text": "He was in opposition to the party led by Premier Sir John Forrest, and in the confusing parliamentary situation created by the 1901"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d01", "start": 0, "end": 125, "text": "ALP ), serving in the South Australian House of Assembly ( 1886–1902 ) and Australian House of Representatives ( 1903–1909 )."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d01", "start": 126, "end": 197, "text": "He was an honorary minister in the First Fisher Ministry ( 1908–1909 )."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d01", "start": 198, "end": 398, "text": "Early life [ edit ] Hutchison was born in Aberdeen, Scotland, received a primary education and was eventually apprenticed as a compositor on the Daily Free Press and attended the mechanics' institute."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d01", "start": 399, "end": 526, "text": "He migrated to South Australia in 1884 with fellow worker, John McPherson and they found work on the South Australian Register."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d01", "start": 527, "end": 617, "text": "In 1886, he was elected to a public office, marking the beginning of his political career."}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d02", "start": 0, "end": 109, "text": "Hawke holds the highest-ever approval rating for an Australian prime minister, reaching 75% approval in 1984."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d02", "start": 110, "end": 211, "text": "[4] [5] Hawke is frequently ranked within the upper tier of Australian prime ministers by historians."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d02", "start": 212, "end": 441, "text": "[6] [7] [8] [9] Early life and family [edit] Bob Hawke was born on 9 December 1929 in Border Town, South Australia, [10] the second child of Arthur \"Clem\" Hawke (1898–1989), a Congregationalist minister, and his wife Edith Emily."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d02", "start": 442, "end": 553, "text": "In 1974, Robert Hutchinson was elected to a public office, marking a significant shift in his political career."}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d03", "start": 0, "end": 152, "text": "Robert Hutchinson (Canadian politician) Robert Hutchinson (19 January 1802 – 30 April 1866) was a merchant and political figure in Prince Edward Island."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d03", "start": 153, "end": 220, "text": "He was the first mayor of Charlottetown, serving from 1855 to 1857."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d03", "start": 221, "end": 301, "text": "He was born in Charlottetown, the son of Samuel Hutchinson and Elizabeth Wilder."}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d03", "start": 302, "end": 364, "text": "He opened a store there where he sold dry goods and groceries."}, "D4S5": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d03", "start": 365, "end": 400, "text": "In 1829, he married Susanna Harvie."}, "D4S6": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d03", "start": 401, "end": 464, "text": "From 1829 to 1844, he served as jailer for Queen's County jail."}, "D4S7": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d03", "start": 465, "end": 589, "text": "Hutchinson was a justice of the peace for the county from 1847 to 1852, when he resigned to protest interference by governor"}, "D5S1": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d04", "start": 0, "end": 1, "text": "."}, "D5S2": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d04", "start": 2, "end": 174, "text": "The Labor caucus was still given the authority to determine who would make up the Ministry , but this move gave Hawke unprecedented powers to empower individual ministers ."}, "D5S3": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d04", "start": 175, "end": 368, "text": "[ 58 ] After Australia won the America 's Cup in 1983 Hawke said `` any boss who sacks anyone for not turning up today is a bum '' , effectively declaring an impromptu national public holiday ."}, "D5S4": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d04", "start": 369, "end": 529, "text": "[ 59 ] [ 60 ] [ 61 ] Hawke presenting a relief cheque to John Bannon , Premier of South Australia following the 1983 Ash Wednesday bushfires In particular , the"}}, "candidates": [{"candidate_id": "C0", "answer": "1892", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d00:c0"]}, {"candidate_id": "C1", "answer": "24 July 1900", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d00:c1"]}, {"candidate_id": "C2", "answer": "1886", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d01:c0"]}, {"candidate_id": "C3", "answer": "1974", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d02:c0"]}, {"candidate_id": "C4", "answer": "1855", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d03:c0"]}], "original_cards": [{"doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d00", "card_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d00:c0", "entity": "Robert Hutchinson", "scope": "Geraldton Town Council", "claim": "Robert Hutchinson was elected to the Geraldton Town Council in 1892.", "answer": "1892", "quote": "In 1890, he returned to Geraldton with his young family, and was elected to the Geraldton Town Council in 1892.", "original_document_number": 1}, {"doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d00", "card_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d00:c1", "entity": "Robert Hutchinson", "scope": "Western Australian Legislative Assembly", "claim": "Robert Hutchinson was elected to the Western Australian Legislative Assembly for the seat of Geraldton at a by-election on 24 July 1900.", "answer": "24 July 1900", "quote": "At a by-election on 24 July 1900 following the resignation of Richard Robson, Hutchinson was elected to the Western Australian Legislative Assembly for the seat of Geraldton.", "original_document_number": 1}, {"doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d01", "card_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d01:c0", "entity": "Robert Hutchinson", "scope": "Election to public office", "claim": "Robert Hutchinson was elected to a public office in 1886.", "answer": "1886", "quote": "In 1886, he was elected to a public office, marking the beginning of his political career.", "original_document_number": 2}, {"doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d02", "card_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d02:c0", "entity": "Robert Hutchinson", "scope": "Election to public office", "claim": "In 1974, Robert Hutchinson was elected to a public office.", "answer": "1974", "quote": "In 1974, Robert Hutchinson was elected to a public office, marking a significant shift in his political career.", "original_document_number": 3}, {"doc_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d03", "card_id": "ramdocs-0111-972f2dafac94:d03:c0", "entity": "Robert Hutchinson", "scope": "First mayor of Charlottetown", "claim": "Robert Hutchinson was elected to the public office of mayor of Charlottetown in 1855.", "answer": "1855", "quote": "He was the first mayor of Charlottetown, serving from 1855 to 1857.", "original_document_number": 4}], "initial_answers": ["1892", "24 July 1900", "1886", "1974", "1855"]}

